{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791602564,"nonce":"PGZYMp1SdFwfKHpryXqyPtiw6mo=","title":"HirType clones pointer types deeply on every value","message":"HirType::Ptr, Ref and Array boxed their element, so cloning a pointer to a struct copied the struct's whole field list, recursively, and every SSA value, local and instruction type is a clone. A Python go start-up profile (ZYPY_PROFILE_STARTUP=400, Apple M-series) had about 11% of its samples in HirType clone and drop (406 clone, 360 drop, 155 Vec\u003cHirType\u003e clone of 7177), a quarter of them inside the SSA builder.\n\nThe element of Ptr, Ref and Array is now an Arc: a clone is a reference count. Serialisation is unchanged (serde's rc feature writes an Arc as its value).\n\nStart-up instructions against 5bc7c549 (run stopped once start-up is done, PROFILE_STARTUP=10, median of 3):\n- Python go/nbody/richards/float/json/fft/raytrace/deltablue: 41.98G -\u003e 40.72G (-3.0%); deltablue -6.0%, go -3.5%, richards -3.2%.\n- Lua 17 benchmarks: 29.21G -\u003e 28.16G (-3.6%); nbody -5.7%, op_overload -5.0%, method_call -4.9%.\nAfter the change the same go profile has 65 samples in HirType clone.","files":null}]}